<section xml:id="sec-longitudes-and-latitudes" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Longitudes and Latitudes</title>
    <objectives component="outcomes">
        <introduction>
            <p>
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        2.0 Measurements and Geometry
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Sub-Strand</title>
                    <p component="core">
                        2.7 Navigation
                    </p>
                    <p component="essential">
                        2.3 Navigation
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Specific Learning Outcomes</title> 
                    <p component="core">
                        Identify longitudes and latitude in different situations; Locate points on the earth surface using latitudes and longitudes
                    </p>
                    <p component="essential">
                        Identify longitudes and latitudes in different resources; Locate points on the earth surface using latitudes and longitudes
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Grade Level</title>
                    <p>
                        12
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Estimated Duration</title>
                    <p>
                        2 lessons of 40 minutes
                    </p>
                </li>
            </dl>
            </p>
        </introduction>
    </objectives>

    <!-- Planning notes (from the Combined Mathematics tab of the Grade 12 Book Structure Mapping sheet)
         Sheet title: Longitudes and Latitudes (Small circles, Great circles, Location of points on the earth surface)
         Content summary: Latitudes and longitudes; small and great circles; locating points on the earth's surface by coordinates.
         Suggested activity: Learners use a globe or an orange marked with rubber bands to identify the equator, parallels of latitude and meridians, and locate Nairobi, Mombasa and Kisumu by coordinates.
         Interactive (JSXGraph): Rotatable globe with lines of latitude and longitude; a draggable point shows its latitude and longitude. -->

    <!-- Section notes (2026-11)
         SLOs: Core (a), (b); Essential (a), (b). Lessons 1.1 and 1.2 as subsections.
         Activity: as planning notes (an orange with rubber bands in 1.1; locating Nairobi, Mombasa, Kisumu and other towns on a globe in 1.2)
         JSXGraph: fig-globe-great-and-small-circles (assets/jsxgraph/globe-great-and-small-circles.js) and fig-globe-latitude-longitude (assets/jsxgraph/globe-latitude-longitude.js) | changed: latitude and longitude sliders instead of a draggable point (a JSXGraph 1.8.0 3D point drags only within a plane, as Ch12); the view sliders turn the globe
         STACK: assets/stack/top/2-measurement-and-geometry/13-navigation/01-longitudes-and-latitudes/ -->

    <introduction>
        <question xml:id="kiq-navigation">
            <p><em>How do we use navigation in daily life?</em></p>
        </question>
        <p>
            Every day ships sail into the ports of Mombasa and Lamu, Kenya Airways flights leave Nairobi for cities on every continent, and fishermen on Lake Victoria find their way back to the beach they left before dawn. Matatu and boda boda riders follow maps on their phones to reach an address they have never visited. All of them need to know where they are, where they are going, how far it is and how long it will take.
        </p>
        <p>
            This chapter describes positions on the earth by two angles, latitude and longitude. From those angles you will find distances along the earth's surface in kilometres and in nautical miles, speeds in knots, and the local time at places east and west of you. As you work, look out for the ways people around you use these ideas.
        </p>
        <note xml:id="note-earth-constants">
            <title>Values Used in This Chapter</title>
            <p>
                <ul>
                    <li><p>The earth is treated as a sphere. (The real earth bulges a little at the equator: measured to the equator it is wider than measured through the poles, by about <m>1</m> part in <m>300</m>. We ignore this.)</p></li>
                    <li><p>The radius of the earth is <m>R = 6370\,\text{km}</m>. Its mean radius is about <m>6371\,\text{km}</m>; the value <m>6370\,\text{km}</m> is the one used in Kenyan secondary schools.</p></li>
                    <li><p>We take <m>\pi = \tfrac{22}{7}</m>. Then the length of a great circle is <m>2\pi R = 2 \times \tfrac{22}{7} \times 6370 = 40\,040\,\text{km}</m> exactly, and <m>1^\circ</m> of a great circle is <m>40\,040 \div 360 = 111.2\,\text{km}</m>.</p></li>
                    <li><p>One nautical mile is <m>1.853\,\text{km}</m> and one knot is one nautical mile per hour (<xref ref="sec-distances-on-the-earths-surface"/> and <xref ref="sec-distance-and-speed"/>).</p></li>
                </ul>
            </p>
        </note>
    </introduction>

    <!-- ============================================================ Lesson 1.1 -->
    <subsection xml:id="subsec-great-and-small-circles">
        <title>Great and Small Circles</title>

        <p component="lesson-plan">Upon completion of this lesson, learners will be able to:
            <ol>
                <li><p><alert>Know:</alert> a plane cuts a sphere in a circle; a great circle has its plane through the centre and the same radius as the sphere; any other circle is a small circle; the shortest path along the surface between two points follows a great circle.</p></li>
                <li><p><alert>Do:</alert> find the radius of a small circle from the radius of the sphere and the distance of the plane from the centre; classify circles on the earth as great or small.</p></li>
                <li><p><alert>Apply:</alert> recognise the equator, parallels of latitude and pairs of meridians as great or small circles.</p></li>
            </ol>
        </p>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
            <dl>
                <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>14 minutes</p></li>
                <li><title>Objective</title><p>Learners discover that every plane section of a sphere is a circle, that the largest such circles pass through the centre, and that a thread stretched tightly between two points on the surface follows one of them.</p></li>
                <li><title>Teacher's Role</title><p>Give each group an orange (or a ball), two rubber bands, two pins and a piece of thread; keep a globe at the front. The teacher makes the cut in part (b). Do not use the words <q>great circle</q> until after part (c).</p></li>
            </dl>
        </axiom>

        <activity xml:id="act-circles-on-an-orange">
            <title>Circles on an Orange</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>Put a rubber band round the orange so that it divides the orange into two equal halves. Put a second band round it nearer the top. Which band is longer? Why?</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>The first band. It goes round the widest part of the orange, in a plane through the centre.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>The teacher cuts an orange of radius <m>4\,\text{cm}</m> with one straight cut <m>2.4\,\text{cm}</m> from its centre. Predict the radius of the cut face, then measure it.</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>The centre of the orange, the centre of the cut face and a point on the edge of the cut form a right-angled triangle with hypotenuse <m>4\,\text{cm}</m>. So <m>r^2 = 4^2 - 2.4^2 = 10.24</m> and <m>r = 3.2\,\text{cm}</m>.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Push two pins into the peel and stretch a thread tightly between them along the surface. Now hold a band so that it passes through both pins and divides the orange into two equal halves. What do you notice?</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>The thread lies along the band. The shortest path between the pins follows the circle whose plane passes through the centre of the orange.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>On the globe, which of these divide the earth into two equal halves: the equator; the circle of latitude through Nairobi; the Greenwich meridian together with the <m>180^\circ</m> meridian?</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>The equator, and the Greenwich meridian with the <m>180^\circ</m> meridian. The circle through Nairobi lies just south of the equator, so its plane does not pass through the centre.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </activity>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Structured Instruction</title>
            <p>
                <dl>
                    <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>8 minutes</p></li>
                    <li><title>Objective</title><p>To name great and small circles and find the radius of a small circle by Pythagoras.</p></li>
                </dl>
            </p>
            <paragraphs>
                <title>Key Takeaways &amp; Teacher Connection</title>
                <ol>
                    <li><p><alert>Connecting to Learner Work:</alert> the first band in part (a) and the band in part (c) lie on great circles; the second band in part (a) and the cut in part (b) are small circles.</p></li>
                    <li><p>A plane through the centre gives the biggest circle, with the radius of the sphere.</p></li>
                    <li><p>For any other plane, Pythagoras in the triangle of part (b) gives the radius.</p></li>
                    <li><p>A single meridian runs from pole to pole on one side of the earth: it is half of a great circle.</p></li>
                </ol>
            </paragraphs>
        </axiom>

        <insight xml:id="insight-great-and-small-circles">
            <title>Great and Small Circles</title>
            <p>
                A <term>sphere</term> is the set of points in space at the same distance <m>R</m> from a centre <m>O</m>. Any plane that cuts a sphere cuts it in a circle.
            </p>
            <p>
                <ul>
                    <li><p>If the plane passes through the centre, the circle is a <term>great circle</term>: its radius is the radius <m>R</m> of the sphere.</p></li>
                    <li><p>If the plane is at a distance <m>d</m> from the centre, with <m>0 \lt d \lt R</m>, the circle is a <term>small circle</term>. Its radius <m>r</m> satisfies <md>r^2 = R^2 - d^2</md>, from the right-angled triangle in <xref ref="fig-great-and-small-circles"/>.</p></li>
                </ul>
            </p>
            <p>
                The shortest distance between two points on the surface of a sphere is along the shorter arc of the great circle through them.
            </p>
        </insight>

        <figure xml:id="fig-great-and-small-circles">
            <caption>A great circle through the centre <m>O</m>, and a small circle a distance <m>d</m> above it. The triangle has sides <m>d</m>, <m>r</m> and hypotenuse <m>R</m>.</caption>
