        <figure xml:id="fig-globe-great-and-small-circles">
            <caption>A plane cutting a globe. Move the <m>h</m> slider to slide the plane up and down; the thick circle is where it cuts the sphere.</caption>
            <interactive xml:id="interactive-globe-great-and-small-circles" platform="jsxgraph" width="90%" aspect="16:12" source="jsxgraph/globe-great-and-small-circles.js">
                <slate xml:id="jsxgraph-globe-great-and-small-circles" surface="jsxboard" aspect="16:12"/>
                <instructions>
                    <p>
                        <ul>
                            <li><p>Move the <alert>h</alert> slider to slide a plane through the globe. The sphere has radius <m>1</m>; <m>h</m> is the distance of the plane above or below the centre.</p></li>
                            <li><p>The circle where the plane cuts is largest when the plane passes through the centre (<m>h = 0</m>). Check that <m>h = 0.35</m> gives <m>r = 0.94</m>, which on the earth is about <m>5967\,\text{km}</m>.</p></li>
                            <li><p>Turn the globe with the two view sliders at the bottom and left of the board.</p></li>
                        </ul>
                    </p>
                </instructions>
            </interactive>
        </figure>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

        <example xml:id="example-slicing-a-watermelon">
            <title>Slicing a Watermelon</title>
            <statement>
                <p>A watermelon from a farm in Makueni is a sphere of radius <m>13\,\text{cm}</m>. It is cut with one straight cut <m>5\,\text{cm}</m> from its centre. Find the radius of the cut face.</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>The centre of the watermelon, the centre of the cut face and a point on the edge of the cut make a right-angled triangle with hypotenuse <m>13\,\text{cm}</m>:
                    <md>r^2 = 13^2 - 5^2 = 169 - 25 = 144</md>
                    so <m>r = 12\,\text{cm}</m>.</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="example-great-or-small">
            <title>Great or Small?</title>
            <statement>
                <p>Say whether each of these is a great circle, a small circle or half of a great circle.
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li><p>The equator.</p></li>
                        <li><p>The parallel of latitude <m>23^\circ 26'\,\text{S}</m> (the Tropic of Capricorn).</p></li>
                        <li><p>The meridian <m>37^\circ\,\text{E}</m>.</p></li>
                        <li><p>The meridian <m>37^\circ\,\text{E}</m> together with the meridian <m>143^\circ\,\text{W}</m>.</p></li>
                        <li><p>The circle traced by Mombasa as the earth turns once on its axis.</p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li><p>Great circle: its plane passes through the centre of the earth.</p></li>
                        <li><p>Small circle: its plane is parallel to the equator but below the centre.</p></li>
                        <li><p>Half of a great circle: a meridian runs from pole to pole on one side of the earth only.</p></li>
                        <li><p>Great circle: <m>37 + 143 = 180</m>, so the two meridians are opposite each other and together make a whole circle through the centre.</p></li>
                        <li><p>Small circle: Mombasa moves round its own parallel of latitude, which is not the equator.</p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="example-how-far-from-the-centre-core" component="core">
            <title>How Far from the Centre?</title>
            <statement>
                <p>A small circle of radius <m>7.7\,\text{cm}</m> is drawn on a ball of radius <m>8.5\,\text{cm}</m>. How far is the plane of the circle from the centre of the ball?</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>In the right-angled triangle of <xref ref="fig-great-and-small-circles"/> the hypotenuse is <m>8.5</m> and one side is <m>7.7</m>:
                    <md>d^2 = 8.5^2 - 7.7^2 = 72.25 - 59.29 = 12.96</md>
                    so <m>d = 3.6\,\text{cm}</m>. The same triangle gives the radius of a parallel of latitude on the earth (<xref ref="subsec-distance-along-a-parallel"/>).</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />
        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/checkpoint.ptx" />

        <p>Type decimals with a point, for example <c>36.8</c>. Type angles in degrees as numbers, without the degree sign. Choose N, S, E or W from each list.</p>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-great-circle-or-small-circle">
            <!--<title></title>-->
            <stack label="stk-great-circle-or-small-circle" source="stack/top/2-measurement-and-geometry/13-navigation/01-longitudes-and-latitudes/Great-circle-or-small-circle.xml"/>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-radius-of-a-small-circle">
            <!--<title></title>-->
            <stack label="stk-radius-of-a-small-circle" source="stack/top/2-measurement-and-geometry/13-navigation/01-longitudes-and-latitudes/Radius-of-a-small-circle.xml"/>
        </exercise>

        <exercises xml:id="exercises-great-and-small-circles">
            <exercise xml:id="ex-small-circle-radius-ball">
                <statement><p>A ball has radius <m>17\,\text{cm}</m>. A plane cuts it <m>8\,\text{cm}</m> from the centre. Find the radius of the circle of the cut.</p></statement>
                <answer><p><m>15\,\text{cm}</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p><m>r^2 = 17^2 - 8^2 = 289 - 64 = 225</m>, so <m>r = 15\,\text{cm}</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-small-circle-distance">
                <statement><p>A small circle of radius <m>24\,\text{cm}</m> is drawn on a sphere of radius <m>25\,\text{cm}</m>. How far is the plane of the circle from the centre of the sphere?</p></statement>
                <answer><p><m>7\,\text{cm}</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p><m>d^2 = 25^2 - 24^2 = 625 - 576 = 49</m>, so <m>d = 7\,\text{cm}</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-classify-five-circles">
                <statement>
                    <p>Say whether each is a great circle, a small circle or half of a great circle.
                        <ol marker="(a)">
                            <li><p>The parallel of latitude <m>1^\circ 17'\,\text{S}</m>.</p></li>
                            <li><p>The Greenwich meridian.</p></li>
                            <li><p>The Greenwich meridian together with the <m>180^\circ</m> meridian.</p></li>
                            <li><p>The parallel of latitude <m>0^\circ</m>.</p></li>
                            <li><p>A circle on the earth's surface of radius <m>3000\,\text{km}</m>.</p></li>
                        </ol>
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <answer><p>(a) small; (b) half of a great circle; (c) great; (d) great (it is the equator); (e) small</p></answer>
                <solution><p>A great circle has its centre at the centre of the earth. (a) A parallel other than the equator has its centre on the axis above or below the earth's centre: small. (b) A meridian runs from pole to pole only: half of a great circle. (c) The two meridians together make a whole circle through both poles: great. (d) The equator: great. (e) A great circle has radius <m>R = 6370\,\text{km}</m>; <m>3000\,\text{km}</m> is less, so small.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-football-in-a-ring">
                <statement><p>A football of radius <m>11\,\text{cm}</m> rests in a circular ring of radius <m>8\,\text{cm}</m>, touching it all the way round. How high is the centre of the ball above the plane of the ring? Give your answer correct to 2 decimal places.</p></statement>
                <answer><p><m>\sqrt{57} = 7.55\,\text{cm}</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p>The ring is a small circle of the ball. <m>d^2 = 11^2 - 8^2 = 121 - 64 = 57</m>, so <m>d = \sqrt{57} = 7.55\,\text{cm}</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-great-circles-meet-at-opposite-points">
                <title>Challenge: Where Great Circles Meet</title>
                <statement><p>Explain why any two different great circles on a sphere cross at exactly two points, and why those two points are at opposite ends of a diameter.</p></statement>
                <hint><p>Both planes pass through the centre.</p></hint>
                <answer><p>The two planes meet in a line through the centre, which meets the sphere at the two ends of a diameter.</p></answer>
                <solution><p>The planes of the two great circles are different, and both contain the centre <m>O</m>. Two different planes that share a point meet in a straight line, so they meet in a line through <m>O</m>. The circles cross where this line meets the sphere: at two points, each a distance <m>R</m> from <m>O</m> on opposite sides of it, that is, at the two ends of a diameter.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
        </exercises>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Differentiation</title>
            <table xml:id="table-differentiation-great-and-small-circles">
                <title>Differentiation Strategies</title>
                <tabular halign="left" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                    <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Learner Group</cell><cell>Strategy and Learning Experience</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Struggling Learners (Support)</cell><cell>Activity parts (a) to (c) and <xref ref="example-slicing-a-watermelon"/>. Give a card with the triangle <q>centre, centre of the cut, edge of the cut</q> and <m>r^2 = R^2 - d^2</m>. Assign <xref ref="ex-small-circle-radius-ball"/> and <xref ref="ex-classify-five-circles"/>.</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>On-Level Learners (Core)</cell><cell>Full activity and all examples. Assign <xref ref="ex-small-circle-radius-ball"/> to <xref ref="ex-football-in-a-ring"/>.</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Advanced Learners (Challenge)</cell><cell>All exercises including <xref ref="ex-great-circles-meet-at-opposite-points"/>. Extension: how many great circles pass through two points that are not opposite each other? Through two opposite points? (Answer: exactly one; infinitely many.)</cell></row>
                </tabular>
            </table>
        </axiom>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-methods.ptx" />
        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/reflection.ptx"/>
    </subsection>

    <!-- ============================================================ Lesson 1.2 -->
    <subsection xml:id="subsec-latitude-and-longitude">
        <title>Latitude, Longitude and Position</title>

        <p component="lesson-plan">Upon completion of this lesson, learners will be able to:
            <ol>
                <li><p><alert>Know:</alert> the poles, the equator, meridians and the Greenwich meridian, parallels of latitude; latitude and longitude as angles at the centre of the earth; degrees and minutes.</p></li>
                <li><p><alert>Do:</alert> read and write positions; find differences in latitude and in longitude; find the point on the opposite side of the earth.</p></li>
                <li><p><alert>Apply:</alert> locate Kenyan and African towns by their positions; read positions from a phone.</p></li>
            </ol>
        </p>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
            <dl>
                <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>14 minutes</p></li>
                <li><title>Objective</title><p>Learners locate towns from their latitude and longitude and discover when to add and when to subtract to find a difference in latitude or longitude.</p></li>
                <li><title>Teacher's Role</title><p>Give each group a globe or an atlas, or use the interactive in <xref ref="fig-globe-latitude-longitude"/>. Write the five positions on the board. Ask: <q>Why must we always say N or S, and E or W?</q></p></li>
            </dl>
        </axiom>

        <activity xml:id="act-finding-kenya-on-the-globe">
            <title>Finding Kenya on the Globe</title>
            <statement>
                <p>Take Nairobi to be at <m>(1^\circ 17'\,\text{S},\ 36^\circ 49'\,\text{E})</m>, Mombasa at <m>(4^\circ 03'\,\text{S},\ 39^\circ 40'\,\text{E})</m>, Kisumu at <m>(0^\circ 06'\,\text{S},\ 34^\circ 45'\,\text{E})</m>, Lodwar at <m>(3^\circ 07'\,\text{N},\ 35^\circ 36'\,\text{E})</m> and Mandera at <m>(3^\circ 56'\,\text{N},\ 41^\circ 52'\,\text{E})</m>.</p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>Find each town on the globe or on the interactive. Which of them are north of the equator?</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>Lodwar and Mandera.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Which town is furthest east?</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>Mandera (<m>41^\circ 52'\,\text{E}</m>).</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>How many degrees and minutes of latitude separate Lodwar and Nairobi?</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>They are on opposite sides of the equator, so add: <m>3^\circ 07' + 1^\circ 17' = 4^\circ 24'</m>.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>How many degrees and minutes of longitude separate Kisumu and Mombasa?</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>Both are east of Greenwich, so subtract: <m>39^\circ 40' - 34^\circ 45' = 38^\circ 100' - 34^\circ 45' = 4^\circ 55'</m>.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </activity>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Structured Instruction</title>
            <p>
                <dl>
                    <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>8 minutes</p></li>
                    <li><title>Objective</title><p>To define latitude and longitude as angles at the centre and state the rule for differences.</p></li>
                </dl>
            </p>
            <paragraphs>
                <title>Key Takeaways &amp; Teacher Connection</title>
                <ol>
                    <li><p><alert>Connecting to Learner Work:</alert> in part (c) the equator lies between the two towns, so the latitudes add; in part (d) both towns are east of Greenwich, so the longitudes subtract.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Latitude and longitude are angles at the centre of the earth, not distances.</p></li>
                    <li><p>N or S and E or W are part of every position: <m>1^\circ\,\text{S}</m> and <m>1^\circ\,\text{N}</m> are about <m>222\,\text{km}</m> apart.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Same side of the equator (or of Greenwich): subtract. Opposite sides: add.</p></li>
                </ol>
            </paragraphs>
        </axiom>

        <insight xml:id="insight-latitude-and-longitude">
            <title>Latitude and Longitude</title>
            <p>
                The earth turns about an <term>axis</term> through the <term>North Pole</term> and the <term>South Pole</term>. The <term>equator</term> is the great circle halfway between the poles.
            </p>
            <p>
                <ul>
                    <li><p>A <term>meridian</term> is half of a great circle from the North Pole to the South Pole. The meridian through Greenwich in London is the <term>Greenwich meridian</term>, <m>0^\circ</m>.</p></li>
                    <li><p>The <term>longitude</term> of a point P is the angle between the plane of P's meridian and the plane of the Greenwich meridian, from <m>0^\circ</m> to <m>180^\circ</m> east (E) or west (W).</p></li>
                    <li><p>A <term>parallel of latitude</term> is a circle on the earth parallel to the equator. Every parallel except the equator itself is a small circle.</p></li>
                    <li><p>The <term>latitude</term> of P is the angle at the centre between OP and the plane of the equator, measured in P's meridian, from <m>0^\circ</m> to <m>90^\circ</m> north (N) or south (S).</p></li>
                </ul>
            </p>
            <p>
                The <term>position</term> of a point is written (latitude, longitude), latitude first: Nairobi is at about <m>(1^\circ 17'\,\text{S},\ 36^\circ 49'\,\text{E})</m>. Each degree is divided into <m>60</m> minutes: <m>1^\circ = 60'</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                To find the difference in latitude of two points: if they are on the same side of the equator, subtract their latitudes; if they are on opposite sides, add them. Longitudes work the same way with Greenwich in place of the equator; if the sum is more than <m>180^\circ</m>, the smaller angle between the meridians is <m>360^\circ</m> minus the sum (going the shorter way round, across the <m>180^\circ</m> meridian).
            </p>
        </insight>

        <figure xml:id="fig-latitude-and-longitude-angles">
            <caption>The latitude <m>\phi</m> and longitude <m>\lambda</m> of a point P.</caption>
            <image width="65%">
