            </image>
        </figure>

        <note xml:id="note-gps-coordinates">
            <title>Location on Your Phone</title>
            <p>
                Map apps on phones show positions in decimal degrees, for example <c>-1.2864, 36.8172</c> in Nairobi. A minus sign means south (for latitude) or west (for longitude). To change the decimal part to minutes, multiply by <m>60</m>: <m>0.2864 \times 60 = 17.2</m>, so the latitude is <m>1^\circ 17'\,\text{S}</m>; <m>0.8172 \times 60 = 49.0</m>, so the longitude is <m>36^\circ 49'\,\text{E}</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                A phone that shares your live location tells others exactly where you are. Think before you share it, and share it only with people you trust.
            </p>
        </note>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

        <example xml:id="example-differences-in-latitude-and-longitude">
            <title>Differences in Latitude and Longitude</title>
            <statement>
                <p>Take Addis Ababa to be at <m>(9^\circ 02'\,\text{N},\ 38^\circ 45'\,\text{E})</m>, Dar es Salaam at <m>(6^\circ 48'\,\text{S},\ 39^\circ 17'\,\text{E})</m> and Accra at <m>(5^\circ 33'\,\text{N},\ 0^\circ 12'\,\text{W})</m>.
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li><p>Find the differences in latitude and in longitude of Addis Ababa and Dar es Salaam.</p></li>
                        <li><p>Find the difference in longitude of Accra and Nairobi <m>(1^\circ 17'\,\text{S},\ 36^\circ 49'\,\text{E})</m>.</p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li><p>Latitude: one is north and one south of the equator, so add: <m>9^\circ 02' + 6^\circ 48' = 15^\circ 50'</m>. Longitude: both are east, so subtract. The minutes <m>17' - 45'</m> do not subtract, so borrow <m>1^\circ = 60'</m>:
                            <md>39^\circ 17' - 38^\circ 45' = 38^\circ 77' - 38^\circ 45' = 0^\circ 32'</md></p></li>
                        <li><p>Accra is west of Greenwich and Nairobi east, so add: <m>0^\circ 12' + 36^\circ 49' = 36^\circ 61' = 37^\circ 01'</m>.</p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="example-opposite-side-of-the-earth">
            <title>The Opposite Side of the Earth</title>
            <statement>
                <p>Find the position of the point at the other end of the earth's diameter through Nairobi <m>(1^\circ 17'\,\text{S},\ 36^\circ 49'\,\text{E})</m>.</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>The diameter passes through the centre, so the opposite point is as far north of the equator as Nairobi is south: latitude <m>1^\circ 17'\,\text{N}</m>.</p>
                <p>Its meridian is opposite Nairobi's, so the two longitudes add to <m>180^\circ</m>, on the other side of Greenwich: <m>180^\circ - 36^\circ 49' = 143^\circ 11'</m>, so <m>143^\circ 11'\,\text{W}</m>.</p>
                <p>The point is <m>(1^\circ 17'\,\text{N},\ 143^\circ 11'\,\text{W})</m>, in the Pacific Ocean.</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />
        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/checkpoint.ptx" />

        <p>Type decimals with a point, for example <c>36.8</c>. Type angles in degrees as numbers, without the degree sign. Choose N, S, E or W from each list.</p>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-differences-in-latitude-and-longitude">
            <!--<title></title>-->
            <stack label="stk-differences-in-latitude-and-longitude" source="stack/top/2-measurement-and-geometry/13-navigation/01-longitudes-and-latitudes/Differences-in-latitude-and-longitude.xml"/>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-a-new-position-after-moving">
            <!--<title></title>-->
            <stack label="stk-a-new-position-after-moving" source="stack/top/2-measurement-and-geometry/13-navigation/01-longitudes-and-latitudes/A-new-position-after-moving.xml"/>
        </exercise>

        <exercises xml:id="exercises-latitude-and-longitude">
            <exercise xml:id="ex-differences-kisumu-lodwar">
                <statement><p>Take Kisumu to be at <m>(0^\circ 06'\,\text{S},\ 34^\circ 45'\,\text{E})</m> and Lodwar at <m>(3^\circ 07'\,\text{N},\ 35^\circ 36'\,\text{E})</m>. Find their difference in latitude and their difference in longitude.</p></statement>
                <answer><p><m>3^\circ 13'</m>; <m>0^\circ 51'</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p>Opposite sides of the equator: <m>0^\circ 06' + 3^\circ 07' = 3^\circ 13'</m>. Both east: <m>35^\circ 36' - 34^\circ 45' = 34^\circ 96' - 34^\circ 45' = 0^\circ 51'</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-differences-kampala-mogadishu">
                <statement><p>Take Kampala to be at <m>(0^\circ 19'\,\text{N},\ 32^\circ 35'\,\text{E})</m> and Mogadishu at <m>(2^\circ 03'\,\text{N},\ 45^\circ 19'\,\text{E})</m>. Find both differences.</p></statement>
                <answer><p>Latitude <m>1^\circ 44'</m>; longitude <m>12^\circ 44'</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p>Both north: <m>2^\circ 03' - 0^\circ 19' = 1^\circ 63' - 0^\circ 19' = 1^\circ 44'</m>. Both east: <m>45^\circ 19' - 32^\circ 35' = 44^\circ 79' - 32^\circ 35' = 12^\circ 44'</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-differences-lima-mombasa">
                <statement><p>Take Lima to be at <m>(12^\circ 03'\,\text{S},\ 77^\circ 03'\,\text{W})</m> and Mombasa at <m>(4^\circ 03'\,\text{S},\ 39^\circ 40'\,\text{E})</m>. Find both differences.</p></statement>
                <answer><p>Latitude <m>8^\circ 00'</m>; longitude <m>116^\circ 43'</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p>Both south: <m>12^\circ 03' - 4^\circ 03' = 8^\circ 00'</m>. Opposite sides of Greenwich: <m>77^\circ 03' + 39^\circ 40' = 116^\circ 43'</m>, which is less than <m>180^\circ</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-opposite-mombasa">
                <statement><p>Give the position of the point at the opposite end of the earth's diameter through Mombasa <m>(4^\circ 03'\,\text{S},\ 39^\circ 40'\,\text{E})</m>.</p></statement>
                <answer><p><m>(4^\circ 03'\,\text{N},\ 140^\circ 20'\,\text{W})</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p>Same latitude in the other hemisphere: <m>4^\circ 03'\,\text{N}</m>. Opposite meridian: <m>180^\circ - 39^\circ 40' = 140^\circ 20'\,\text{W}</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-gps-eldoret">
                <statement><p>A phone in Eldoret shows the position <c>0.5143, 35.2698</c>. Write this position in degrees and minutes (to the nearest minute), with N or S and E or W.</p></statement>
                <answer><p><m>(0^\circ 31'\,\text{N},\ 35^\circ 16'\,\text{E})</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p><m>0.5143 \times 60 = 30.9</m>, so <m>0^\circ 31'</m>; positive, so north. <m>0.2698 \times 60 = 16.2</m>, so <m>35^\circ 16'</m>; positive, so east.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-the-short-way-round">
                <title>Challenge: The Short Way Round</title>
                <statement><p>P is at <m>(20^\circ\,\text{N},\ 150^\circ\,\text{E})</m> and Q is at <m>(20^\circ\,\text{N},\ 150^\circ\,\text{W})</m>. Find their difference in longitude, and explain why it is not <m>300^\circ</m>.</p></statement>
                <hint><p>Go across the <m>180^\circ</m> meridian.</p></hint>
                <answer><p><m>60^\circ</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p>The meridians are on opposite sides of Greenwich, and <m>150 + 150 = 300</m> is more than <m>180</m>. Going the other way, each meridian is <m>30^\circ</m> from the <m>180^\circ</m> meridian, so they are <m>30^\circ + 30^\circ = 60^\circ</m> apart, which is <m>360^\circ - 300^\circ</m>. The difference in longitude is the smaller angle, <m>60^\circ</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
        </exercises>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Differentiation</title>
            <table xml:id="table-differentiation-latitude-and-longitude">
                <title>Differentiation Strategies</title>
                <tabular halign="left" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                    <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Learner Group</cell><cell>Strategy and Learning Experience</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Struggling Learners (Support)</cell><cell>Activity parts (a) to (c) and <xref ref="example-differences-in-latitude-and-longitude"/>. Give a card: <q>Same side: subtract. Opposite sides: add.</q> Assign <xref ref="ex-differences-kisumu-lodwar"/> and <xref ref="ex-differences-kampala-mogadishu"/>.</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>On-Level Learners (Core)</cell><cell>Full activity and all examples. Assign <xref ref="ex-differences-kisumu-lodwar"/> to <xref ref="ex-gps-eldoret"/>.</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Advanced Learners (Challenge)</cell><cell>All exercises including <xref ref="ex-the-short-way-round"/>. Extension: name the point opposite <m>(0^\circ,\ 0^\circ)</m> (answer: <m>(0^\circ,\ 180^\circ)</m>).</cell></row>
                </tabular>
            </table>
        </axiom>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-methods.ptx" />
        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/reflection.ptx"/>
